# A gate is driven by a real `spec-test` sub-run, so the runner makes its usual scratch here:
# `.spec-tmp/` (already ignored repo-wide) and a `fragments/<target>/` tree.
#
# ⚠⚠ **THIS LINE IS LOAD-BEARING, and the rule it protects is GENERAL rather than a list of gates.**
#
# ⛔ **A GATE CASE THAT PASSES MINTS A `.test` HERE** (`SpecTestRunner.checkTestFragment` writes a first
# golden whenever the verdict allows it). So every gate case in this corpus is one of exactly two kinds,
# and which kind it is has to be a deliberate choice:
#
#   • **It FAILS, and therefore mints nothing.** Its pinned expectation is wrong ON PURPOSE and must stay
#     wrong; correcting it starts minting a golden that nothing reads back and no gate asserts on. Every
#     such fixture says so in its own header, and the gate reading it asserts on the FAIL verdict line.
#   • **It PASSES, and its gate OWNS the goldens it mints** — clearing them before it runs and removing
#     them after, so the checkout it ran in is the checkout it found.
#
# The orphaned-golden gate (`HarnessSelfTest.requireOrphanedGoldensAreReported`) is neither: it PLANTS
# `.test` files that no case declares, because the census it proves can only be shown to fire against a
# golden nothing reads, and removes them again.
#
# ⭐ **THE ONE FIXTURE OF THE SECOND KIND IS `drifted-golden/`, and it is the exception rather than the
# rule for a stated reason**: drift is only observable on a case that got far enough to COMPARE a committed
# reference, which a failing case never does. `requireDriftedGoldensAreReported` mints its three goldens,
# corrupts two, requires the run to name them, puts them back, requires silence, and removes all three —
# and it CLEARS this tree before it mints, so a run interrupted anywhere leaves nothing the next run cannot
# repair by itself.
#
# An interrupted run of any of them can leave a file behind, and this line is what keeps that from becoming
# tracked.
fragments/
